Generated DUD-E ABL1 screening inputs (not bundled in this repository)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The prepare.py script downloads the source-prepared 3D actives and decoys from:
https://dude.docking.org/targets/abl1/actives_final.sdf.gz
https://dude.docking.org/targets/abl1/decoys_final.sdf.gz

DUD-E is provided by the Irwin and Shoichet Laboratories at UCSF. Its website
states that it is "free to use" and requests this citation:
Mysinger MM, Carchia M, Irwin JJ, Shoichet BK. Directory of Useful Decoys,
Enhanced (DUD-E): Better Ligands and Decoys for Better Benchmarking.
J. Med. Chem. 2012, 55, 6582-6594. https://doi.org/10.1021/jm300687e
Source terms/copyright: https://dude.docking.org/targets/abl1/
Copyright 2009-2024 The Irwin Lab and the UC Regents, as stated on that page.
No named open-data redistribution license is identified there. The repository's
Apache license does not relicense DUD-E. Archives and generated screening data
remain local runtime artifacts; no DUD-E structures are embedded in this repo.

The 100-compound library is a deterministic demonstration subset (20 labelled
actives and 80 property-matched decoys), not an unbiased prospective screen.
DUD-E source protonation/tautomer states and 3D coordinates are retained for
Meeko preparation. Deduplication uses the neutral parent canonical-tautomer
standard InChIKey connectivity block, so protonation, tautomer and stereochemical
variants do not inflate the count. Normalization is used for identity only,
not to change the source molecule used in docking. Imatinib is excluded from
the library and the bundled 1IEP ligand is added separately as a reference.
Imatinib identity: PubChem CID 5291 / ChEMBL941,
https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/Imatinib

Preparation software references:
https://github.com/rdkit/rdkit/releases/tag/Release_2025_09_3
https://github.com/forlilab/Meeko/releases/tag/v0.6.1
https://apidocmeeko.readthedocs.io/en/latest/lig_overview.html
The manifest records pinned tool versions, source SHA-256 hashes, selected
record identities, prepared file hashes and duplicate/preparation failures.
